+
+Upstream width is therefore chosen by measurement on both bands, not a fixed
+rule (`wifi_relay/width.rs`); the hotspots are never changed by it. The STA
+starts at its negotiated width. On relay-watch ticks (3 s) with at least 1
+Mbit/s of downlink, the AP's `rx bitrate` (it already reflects width, MCS and
+streams) is sampled; four samples give a mean per width (a median hid a flaky
+40 MHz link's failures behind its occasional HE-MCS 11 frames). The next
+narrower width (160, 80, 40, 20 MHz) is tried with `iwpriv u60sta0 chwidth`
+(0-3 = 20-160 MHz, no reassociation) and kept only if at least 15% faster;
+the search stops there and returns to the fastest width. Two busy ticks after
+each change are skipped: the AP's rate control took about 5 s to climb. A
+settled choice is measured again from the widest width after 15 minutes,
+doubling up to 4 hours while the wider width keeps losing and back to 15
+minutes once it wins, or at once if the settled rate halves.
+
+Live (agent `cacb3fa7`, first version: median, one skipped tick), W at
+2.4 GHz with downloads running: the tuner tried 20 MHz while 40 MHz was
+briefly good (109 Mbit/s) and kept 40; when 40 MHz collapsed (15 Mbit/s) it
+searched again and moved to 20 MHz. Over the next 75 s: 40 MHz 8 s, mean AP
+rate 80 Mbit/s, throughput 29; 20 MHz 67 s, AP rate 216, throughput 104.
+
+Live (agent `c5cfcc60`, final rules), 5220 MHz upstream negotiated at 160 MHz,
+downloads running: 160 MHz measured, 80 MHz tried at 16 s (mean AP rate 1113
+against 1745 Mbit/s), back to 160 MHz at 36 s. Throughput stayed 249-251
+Mbit/s in all seven 12 s runs, the trial included (the path's limit). If a reassociation widens the link the choice is re-applied; if the
+link narrows or widens beyond the search range, measurement starts again. Idle
+links are not measured and nothing extra is sent. The relay page shows the
+current band and width.
+
+Start and score (2026-10-28): a search starts at the width settled on last
+time for the same network (opaque scan id, newest 32 kept in
+`/data/zte-agent-relay/widths.json`, 0600). Without one, the scan predicts a
+start: every fresh BSS, hidden ones included, is kept with its frequency and
+signal, and the start is the widest block around the primary that no other
+network at -82 dBm or stronger (the 20 MHz CCA preamble threshold) overlaps;
+networks on the primary itself are ignored. Reviews still try wider widths.
+After association the agent enables qcacld link-layer stats on `u60sta0`
+(QCA vendor command 14 `LL_STATS_SET` over nl80211, sent by the agent:
+`iw vendor send` fails because the kernel wants the data flagged nested) and
+scores a busy tick as delivered downlink bits per second of the STA radio's
+`rx_time` from debugfs `ll_stats`. Failed and repeated frames use airtime
+without delivering, which the last frame's `rx bitrate` hid. On W, 10 s per
+width: 40 MHz gave 0 and 10 Mbit/s with 0 and 42 per airtime-second, 20 MHz 59
+and 90 with 101 and 129. If the stats cannot be read, that session restarts
+measurement on `rx bitrate`; the two scores are never compared. Per-peer
+receive counts by rate are not filled by this firmware (`rxmpdu` 0).
+
+Runtime `hostapd_cli` geometry changes on `wlan2` are unreliable on B31: with
+the relay STA connected even the original 160 MHz configuration and ACS ended
+`DISABLED`. `/etc/init.d/hostapd-daemon.init restart wlan2` (the vendor's own
+per-interface restart) brought it back from its unchanged configuration file.
